{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Research Translation for Learning Design","title":"Reflection as Data","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting reflection as data to the reflection codebook with coded excerpts and decision-linkage trace, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the reflection codebook with coded excerpts and decision-linkage trace shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"05","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/07/04"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_calibration"]},"content":"# Reflection as Data\n\n![Reflection as Data textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/07-05-reflection-as-data.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nLearner reflections are abundant and tempting to use as evidence, but in their raw form most are unusable: \"I liked the lesson and learned a lot\" names no feature, no behavior, and no consequence a designer could act on. Treating reflection as data means subjecting it to the same discipline you would apply to any qualitative source: select an analytic excerpt, apply a defined code, and tie the code to a design decision, while staying honest that self-report measures perception, not measured learning. The move converts a vague compliment into a finding that can drive a specific revision.\n\nThis matters because reflection is often the only \"outcome\" data a small design study has, and how you treat it determines whether it is evidence or decoration. A coded, excerpt-grounded reflection can justify a screen change; an uncoded global impression can justify nothing, no matter how positive it sounds.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish a global impression (uncodeable) from an analytic excerpt that names a feature and a learner action.\n- Apply a defined code to an excerpt and link the code to a specific design decision.\n- Keep the inference modest: treat reflection as evidence of perceived experience, not as a measured learning gain, and pair it with a planned check.\n- Reject coding that reads competence into a reaction.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA learner writes: \"I really liked the lesson and felt like I learned a lot. Great experience.\" It is warm, and it is worthless as design evidence. There is no feature to revise, no behavior to observe, and no way to confirm the perceived learning actually occurred. Treated as a finding, this text would let a designer claim success while changing nothing. The work of this section is to refuse that shortcut: to look for the part of any reflection (if any) that names a concrete feature and a concrete action, code it, and let only that drive a decision.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nReflection becomes data through three disciplined moves, each guarding against a different error:\n\n- **Excerpt selection:** choose the span of text that names a design feature and a learner action, and set aside global praise or blame. The unit of analysis is the codeable claim, not the whole paragraph.\n- **Code application:** assign a defined code whose meaning is stated in advance, so that the same code would be applied the same way by another reader. A code like \"redundancy-noticed (narration duplicates on-screen text)\" has a clear inclusion rule; a code like \"positive\" does not.\n- **Decision linkage:** connect the coded excerpt to one design change you can recheck. A code that changes nothing is a category, not evidence.\n\nThroughout, the inference stays bounded: a reflection is self-report about *perceived* experience. It can tell you a learner noticed redundancy and changed how they read; it cannot tell you their recall improved. Honest practice pairs the reflection-based decision with a planned objective check.\n\nTurning a raw reflection into data follows a fixed order. Arrange the moves.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Select the excerpt: keep only the span that names a feature and a learner action; set global praise aside.\", \"Apply a defined code with a stated inclusion rule another reader could reproduce (redundancy-noticed).\", \"Link the code to one recheckable design decision (remove the duplicated on-screen text on that frame).\", \"Bound the inference: claim perceived experience only, and schedule an objective check (label-recall) before claiming a gain.\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A reflection reads: 'Great lesson, super clear, I felt confident the whole time.' A peer codes it 'mastery-achieved.' What is wrong?\" explanation=\"The text is global praise with no feature or action to code, and confidence is a feeling, not a demonstrated skill. Coding a reaction as mastery reads competence into self-report, which a performance measure would be needed to support.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Nothing; positive sentiment shows the design works.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It over-codes: a confidence reaction is treated as demonstrated competence, and there is no codeable span.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The code is fine, but the inclusion rule should be looser.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe course anchors give the codes their meaning. **Cognitive load theory** supplies a vocabulary of mechanisms (a learner who \"stopped reading and just listened\" is describing the redundancy effect, a recognized source of extraneous load), which lets you name *why* a feature mattered. **Mayer's redundancy principle** turns that observation into a defensible revision (remove duplicated on-screen text where narration carries the content). **UDL learner variability** cautions that one learner's coded experience is not the population's; a redundancy code from a strong reader may not generalize to a learner who relies on the on-screen text, so the revision is provisional and checked across learners.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nTrace one reflection from unusable description to a coded data point that drives a decision.\n\n**Before (raw learner reflection, treated as a finding):**\n\n> \"I really liked the lesson and felt like I learned a lot. Great experience.\"\n\n1. This is global praise with no feature, behavior, or consequence, so it cannot be coded or acted on.\n2. There is no span of text that names a design element a revision could target.\n3. \"Felt like I learned a lot\" is perceived learning, not a measured gain.\n\n**After (coded excerpt with design decision):**\n\n> Excerpt: \"When the narration and the on-screen text repeated each other, I stopped reading and just listened, so I missed the labels.\"\n> Code applied: Redundancy-noticed (narration duplicates on-screen text).\n> Design decision: remove the duplicated on-screen text on that frame; recheck whether label recall improves.\n\nThe revised version turns reflection into data: a defined code grounded in a specific span, tied to a concrete change and a recheck. The inference stays modest.\n\nOne rejected move: writing \"learners learned more.\" I declined because the reflection is self-report about perception, not a measured learning gain, so I paired the revision with a planned recall check instead of claiming an outcome the data cannot support.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A learner writes: 'I kept clicking Run before reading the prompt, then had to start over.' State the codeable excerpt, a defined code with its inclusion rule, the design decision it warrants, and the boundary of what you may claim from it.\" answer=\"Excerpt: the whole sentence, because it names a feature (the Run control) and an action (clicking before reading). Code: premature-action, included when the learner reports acting on a control before reading the instruction. Decision: delay the Run control's activation until the prompt has displayed, and recheck the premature-click rate. Inference boundary: this shows a learner described and reduced a specific friction; it does NOT show learning improved, so pair the change with a behavioral recheck rather than an outcome claim.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore any reflection counts as evidence, run it through three tests:\n\n1. **Excerpt check:** Is there a span that names a feature and a learner action? If only global praise remains, the reflection yields no codeable data.\n2. **Code check:** Does the assigned code have a stated inclusion rule another reader could apply the same way? Replace impressionistic labels with defined codes.\n3. **Linkage-and-modesty check:** Does the code connect to one recheckable design decision, and does the claim stop at perceived experience unless an objective measure backs it?\n\nA reflection that fails the excerpt check is not weak evidence; it is no evidence, and treating it as evidence manufactures false confidence.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading equates positive sentiment with effective design: lots of happy reflections must mean the lesson works. Sentiment and learning routinely diverge, and a lesson can feel smooth while teaching little. The second misreading over-codes, reading competence into a reaction, for example coding \"I felt confident\" as \"mastery achieved.\" Confidence is a feeling, not a demonstrated skill, and calibrating it requires a performance measure. Disciplined coding keeps the code as close to the learner's actual words and actions as possible and resists upgrading reactions into outcomes.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the discipline to a batch of reflections on a simulation lesson. Most say versions of \"it was fun\" (set aside as global). One says: \"I kept clicking Run before reading the prompt, then had to start over.\" That span names a feature (the Run control's prominence) and an action (clicking before reading), so it is codeable as \"premature-action (control invites action before instruction is read).\" The linked design decision is concrete: delay the Run control's activation until the prompt has been displayed, and recheck the premature-click rate. Note what we do *not* claim: that the change \"improves learning.\" We claim it reduces a specific friction the learner described, and we schedule a behavioral recheck. The walkthrough shows the whole arc, from a pile of mostly uncodeable praise to a single coded excerpt that earns a revision.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead each reflection with a highlighter and a discard pile. In the keep pile go only spans that name a feature and an action; everything else, however flattering, goes in the discard pile because it cannot be coded. A strong reader can articulate, for any code they apply, the rule that would tell another coder when the code does and does not fit, and can state the boundary of the inference (\"this shows the learner *noticed* redundancy, not that recall improved\"). The discipline is treating warmth as noise and codeable behavior as signal.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe reflection artifact is strong when each cited reflection is reduced to a defined-code excerpt tied to a recheckable decision, and when the README states the inclusion rule for at least one code and the objective check that will test the perception. Global praise may be reported as context but never as evidence for a design change. Polish of the write-up is the last layer; the load-bearing layer is the excerpt-code-decision chain with an honest inference boundary.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove reflection-as-data into a context with many reflections and a need for reliability, such as coding a full cohort's exit notes. The transfer is to make the codebook shareable: each code gets a definition, an inclusion rule, and an example, so a second coder can apply it and you can check agreement. Scaling forces the discipline you practiced on one excerpt to become explicit, because what stayed in your head with five reflections must be written down for fifty. The skill transfers because it was never about volume; it was about converting subjective text into codes another person could reproduce and act on.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\", \"learner_reflection\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the reflection codebook with coded excerpts and decision-linkage trace packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/07-05-reflection-as-data-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"reflection codebook with coded excerpts and decision-linkage trace\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"07.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong coded reflection excerpt.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the reflection codebook with coded excerpts and decision-linkage trace for AIL 606 07.05. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_reflection_as_data\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the reflection codebook with coded excerpts and decision-linkage trace records codeable-excerpt selection, the defined code with its inclusion rule, the linked design decision, a rejected over-code, and the inference boundary (perceived experience vs measured learning).\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"learner_reflection","stem":"You want to treat learner reflections as data about your design. Which reflection excerpt is analytic enough to code as evidence rather than mere description?","options":["I liked the lesson and learned a lot.","When the narration and the on-screen text repeated each other, I stopped reading and just listened, so I missed the labels.","It was a good experience overall.","The lesson was fine."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Reflection becomes usable data when it names a specific design feature, a behavior, and a consequence. Global praise cannot be coded against a design decision.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_description_as_analysis","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"learner_reflection","stem":"To treat reflections as data, you build a small coding scheme. Which code is defined well enough to apply consistently?","options":["Positive: the learner seemed happy.","Redundancy-noticed: the learner reports that narration and on-screen text duplicated each other.","Engagement: the learner was engaged.","Good: the reflection was good."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A codeable category names an observable claim in the text so two coders could agree. Vague valence labels invite inconsistent coding.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_no_coding_scheme","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to help analyze a set of learner reflections. Which use keeps the analysis trustworthy?","options":["Let the AI assign a satisfaction score to each reflection and report it as the finding.","Ask the AI to suggest candidate codes, then you apply the codebook yourself and spot-check the AI's tags against the raw text.","Have the AI summarize the reflections into one upbeat paragraph for the report.","Ask the AI to predict what learners would have said."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The AI can propose codes, but coding decisions and verification against the source text stay with the analyst. Inventing scores or predicted responses fabricates evidence.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_self_report_as_outcome","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"learner_reflection","stem":"Many reflections say learners felt the simulation \"helped them learn.\" What can you responsibly claim from that pattern?","options":["The simulation caused measurable learning gains.","Learners perceived the simulation as helpful, which is a self-report signal worth pairing with a performance measure before claiming a learning effect.","Nothing; reflections are never useful.","The simulation is effective for all future learners."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Reflection is self-report, evidence of perception, not of measured learning. Treating felt helpfulness as proven gain overstates what the data shows.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_5_reflection_as_data_self_report_as_outcome","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"13fff8f96b8fc48f950d51bb88bf025798cb6075b21f2fe41d2562503899a419","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}